WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.584
Step three.

00:00:00.634 --> 00:00:03.930
We create the identity that GitHub Actions uses to deploy.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.525
There are no passwords and no client secrets.

00:00:06.575 --> 00:00:10.217
GitHub proves who it is with a short-lived OpenID Connect token.

00:00:10.267 --> 00:00:14.000
In the portal search bar, type Managed Identities, and open the service.

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:14.883
Select Create.

00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:20.179
GitHub Actions will sign in as this identity, so no secret is ever stored in GitHub.

00:00:20.229 --> 00:00:23.426
On the Basics tab, pick the resource group we just created.

00:00:23.476 --> 00:00:26.715
Name it ID github contoso health, keep Indonesia Central,

00:00:26.765 --> 00:00:28.389
and select Review and create.

00:00:28.439 --> 00:00:30.918
Review the settings, and select Create.

00:00:30.968 --> 00:00:33.372
The deployment completes in a few seconds.

00:00:33.422 --> 00:00:34.657
Select Go to resource.

00:00:34.707 --> 00:00:37.151
The identity now sits in our resource group.

00:00:37.201 --> 00:00:37.605
Open it.

00:00:37.655 --> 00:00:40.644
On the overview, note the Client ID and the Object ID.

00:00:40.694 --> 00:00:42.670
They go into GitHub variables later.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:45.653
They identify the identity, but they are not secrets.

00:00:47.203 --> 00:00:49.683
Under Settings, open Federated credentials.

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:53.213
This is where Azure learns to trust tokens issued by GitHub.

00:00:53.263 --> 00:00:55.869
Nothing is configured yet. Select Add credential.

00:00:55.919 --> 00:01:00.204
We'll add three: the production environment, pull requests, and the main branch.

00:01:00.254 --> 00:01:04.192
For the scenario, choose GitHub Actions deploying Azure resources.

00:01:04.242 --> 00:01:06.938
The form uses GitHub's immutable subject format,

00:01:06.988 --> 00:01:10.598
which includes the numeric I Ds of the owner and the repository.

00:01:10.648 --> 00:01:13.115
New repositories use this format by default.

00:01:14.165 --> 00:01:17.444
Enter the owner and the repository, with their I Ds.

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:19.364
Both come from the GitHub API.

00:01:19.414 --> 00:01:21.092
For the entity, choose Environment.

00:01:21.142 --> 00:01:24.646
Jobs that declare the production environment will match this credential.

00:01:24.696 --> 00:01:25.509
Type production.

00:01:25.559 --> 00:01:27.883
The subject identifier is generated for you.

00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:31.281
Name the credential, keep the default audience, and select Add.

00:01:31.331 --> 00:01:32.973
One credential is in place.

00:01:33.023 --> 00:01:36.295
Now repeat twice, with the same owner and repository.

00:01:37.845 --> 00:01:40.379
The second credential uses the Pull request entity.

00:01:40.429 --> 00:01:43.115
The Bicep what-if check runs on pull requests with it.

00:01:43.165 --> 00:01:46.227
The third uses the Branch entity, with the value main,

00:01:46.277 --> 00:01:48.013
for image builds and demo jobs.

00:01:48.063 --> 00:01:48.959
Three credentials.

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:51.954
Nothing else in GitHub can get a token for this identity.

00:01:53.004 --> 00:01:54.374
Now we grant permissions.

00:01:54.424 --> 00:01:58.349
Open the resource group, then Access control, and Add role assignment.

00:01:58.399 --> 00:01:59.865
Role one is Contributor.

00:01:59.915 --> 00:02:04.032
It deploys resources, but it cannot grant access or assign policy.

00:02:04.082 --> 00:02:08.090
On Members, choose Managed identity, select members, and pick our identity.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.463
The identity is listed as the member.

00:02:10.513 --> 00:02:12.066
Select Review and assign.

00:02:12.116 --> 00:02:13.196
Check the summary.

00:02:13.246 --> 00:02:16.209
Contributor, scoped to this resource group only.

00:02:16.259 --> 00:02:17.779
Select Review and assign.

00:02:17.829 --> 00:02:19.888
Role two is Resource Policy Contributor.

00:02:19.938 --> 00:02:23.211
It lets the Bicep template create the Azure Policy assignments.

00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:26.014
Role three is Role Based Access Control Administrator.

00:02:26.064 --> 00:02:30.115
Bicep creates two role assignments, so we allow exactly that, and nothing more.

00:02:30.165 --> 00:02:34.397
On Conditions, keep the option that only allows selected roles for selected

00:02:34.447 --> 00:02:36.966
principals. Then select roles and principals.

00:02:37.016 --> 00:02:41.091
Pick the template Constrain roles and principal types, and select Configure.

00:02:41.141 --> 00:02:46.351
Add the role ACR Pull, and then Azure Kubernetes Service RBAC Cluster Admin.

00:02:46.401 --> 00:02:50.249
For principal types, tick Users and Service principals.

00:02:50.299 --> 00:02:52.375
Groups stays off. Select Save.

00:02:52.425 --> 00:02:55.572
The template now shows Configured. Select Save.

00:02:55.622 --> 00:02:57.023
The condition is attached.

00:02:57.073 --> 00:02:59.535
The identity can only assign these two roles,

00:02:59.585 --> 00:03:01.767
and only to users or service principals.

00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:04.933
Review the assignment, and select Review and assign.

00:03:04.983 --> 00:03:07.495
The notification confirms the new assignment.

00:03:07.545 --> 00:03:10.415
On the Role assignments tab, search for the identity.

00:03:10.465 --> 00:03:13.446
Exactly three roles, all scoped to this resource group,

00:03:13.496 --> 00:03:15.320
and nothing at subscription level.
